So the fix adds a check for `GF_M4ASampleRates[ctx->acfg.base_sr_index]` being 0. This is in `latm_dmx_check_pid`. The `GF_M4ASampleRates` array has 16 entries. `base_sr_index` is 4 bits (0-15). So index 15 is within bounds. But the fix checks for value 0, which happens at indices 13, 14, 15.

Wait, but the description says "Stack-buffer-overflow READ". Let me reconsider. Maybe the crash is a READ because of the `memcpy(output, latm_buffer, latm_frame_size)` in `latm_dmx_process`. If `latm_frame_size` (which is `size`) is larger than 4096, then `memcpy` reads `latm_frame_size` bytes from `latm_buffer` (which is only 4096 bytes) → stack-buffer-overflow READ.

But wait, `gf_bs_read_data` would have already written `size` bytes into `latm_buffer`, overflowing it. So the WRITE would be detected first by ASAN.

Hmm, but the description says READ. Let me reconsider.

Actually, wait. Let me reconsider the `latm_dmx_sync_frame_bs` function. Maybe the `size` is not bounded by `gf_bs_available(bs)` in all cases. Let me re-read:

```c
size = 0;
while (1) {
    u32 tmp = gf_bs_read_int(bs, 8);
    size += tmp;
    if (tmp!=255) break;
}
if (gf_bs_available(bs) < size) {
    gf_bs_seek(bs, pos-3);
    return GF_FALSE;
}
```

So `size` is bounded by `gf_bs_available(bs)`. So `size <= gf_bs_available(bs)`. And `gf_bs_read_data` writes `size` bytes into `latm_buffer`. If `size > 4096`, the WRITE overflows `latm_buffer`.

